# Cloudflare Pages response headers.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL: browsers IGNORE `frame-ancestors`, `sandbox` and `report-uri` when a
# CSP arrives in a <meta> tag — they are only honoured as a real HTTP header. Writing them in
# index.html would LOOK like clickjacking protection while providing none, which is worse than
# leaving them out. So they live here, and nowhere else.
#
# THE FULL CSP DELIBERATELY STAYS IN index.html. Two sources of one policy is exactly how CSP bugs
# become unfindable (each policy is enforced independently and the STRICTEST wins, so a stale copy
# here would silently override a corrected one there — with no error, just a dead feature). The
# policy below therefore contains ONE directive: framing. A policy with no `default-src` restricts
# nothing else, so it cannot interfere with index.html's.
#
# Local dev does NOT read this file — Vite ignores it, Cloudflare Pages applies it on deploy. That
# is acceptable precisely because nothing here changes app behaviour: it only forbids things the app
# never does (being framed, MIME sniffing, camera/mic access) — and, at the bottom, explains why there
# is NO caching rule.

/*
  # Clickjacking: this app must never be embedded. `frame-ancestors 'none'` is the modern control;
  # X-Frame-Options repeats it for anything old enough not to honour CSP Level 2.
  Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'none'
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  # Stops a response being re-interpreted as a type it did not declare (the classic way a user
  # upload becomes a script).
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  # The app asks for none of these; saying so means a compromised page cannot ask either.
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=(), usb=()
  # Belt and braces with the <meta> of the same name already in index.html.
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  # HSTS is served by Cloudflare's own edge settings too; declaring it here keeps it true even if
  # that toggle is ever changed. One year, subdomains included, preload-eligible.
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains; preload

# ⚠️ NO long-lived Cache-Control on /assets/* — DELIBERATELY (2026-10-13). Everything here, index.html
# included, keeps Pages' default `max-age=0, must-revalidate` + ETag: the edge still caches it, and a
# browser pays one cheap 304 per file instead of a download. Two production traps removed the old
# `/assets/* … max-age=31536000, immutable` rule:
#   1. A path under /assets/ that is NOT in the current deployment does not 404 — with no top-level
#      404.html, Pages answers it with the SPA fallback (index.html, status 200). The rule stamped that
#      HTML "immutable for a year" under the JS URL. One request landing mid-rollout (new index.html,
#      an edge still serving the previous deployment) therefore bricked the app in that browser for a
#      year: a blank page and "Expected a JavaScript-or-Wasm module script but the server responded
#      with a MIME type of text/html", surviving every normal reload.
#   2. /assets/ is shared with `public/assets` — favicon, manifest, PWA icons, the share image — which
#      are NOT fingerprinted, so a brand change never reached anyone who had visited before.
# A path rule cannot tell a real file from the fallback, so "immutable" here cannot be made safe; it
# needs a status-aware edge rule (Cloudflare response-header Transform Rule on `http.response.code eq
# 200`). Add that in Terraform first if the 304s ever matter — never by restoring the rule above.
